  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 4: ZQ calibration never finished cleanly"
            prompt="This chip's on-die termination is programmed to a perfectly sensible 60 ohm - matched to the board's trace - and Vref was trained carefully, close to the real centre for this channel. The memory test still fails almost every word. Nothing about the *settings* looks wrong by themselves - what haven't you checked yet?"
            hint="A programmed ODT value isn't the same thing as the chip's *actual* termination. Check whether ZQ calibration (against the external 240 ohm reference) actually landed on that target, or drifted well away from it."
            explanation="The ODT target (60 ohm) and the trained Vref are both reasonable - the problem is that ZQ calibration itself never landed cleanly: the chip's real, effective termination ends up significantly off from the 60 ohm the mode register asked for. A mismatched termination reflects a scaled copy of the signal back into the same bit roughly a unit interval later - a real, physical echo, not a wrong number on a register readout - and against a Vref trained close to the edge of its margin (which looked fine for a clean channel), that echo is enough to flip bits almost every time data toggles. This is exactly why real DDR controllers re-run ZQ calibration periodically (including a poll after reset) rather than trusting it once: a *programmed* termination value tells you nothing about whether the calibration that was supposed to realise it in silicon actually worked. The fix is to re-run ZQ cal (here, bring the ZQ cal error back toward 0%), not to change the ODT setting that was already correct."/>
</LABCHAIN>
